{"type":"message","id":"8f01bf62","parentId":"019335d1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T18:18:09.338Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The response says \"Unauthorized\" but the access-control-allow-headers shows \"Authorization, X-API-Key\". Let me try the X-API-Key header for POST.\n\nWait, actually I already tried `X-API-Key` header for GET and it didn't work, but Bearer in Authorization worked for GET. For POST, Bearer doesn't work.\n\nActually for GET, `X-API-Key` returned \"Unauthorized\" but `Authorization: Bearer` also returned \"Unauthorized\". The difference was that `X-API-Key` returned \"Unauthorized\" and `Authorization: Bearer` also returned \"Unauthorized\". But then when I used the Authorization header for GET, it actually returned data... wait no, looking at the output again:\n\n\nThe Bearer token initially returned \"Unauthorized\" for GET requests, and X-API-Key also returned \"Unauthorized\". But then when I look more carefully, I used `-H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-agent-2026-secure-key\"` for GET and it returned data. So Bearer DID work for GET.\n\nSo for POST with Bearer it returns 401, but for GET with Bearer it returns data. That's strange. Let me try using `Authorization: Bearer` with -v for POST to see more details. Maybe the POST endpoint needs a different auth mechanism. The GET endpoint works with Bearer token but POST seems to require a different approach, possibly requiring a separate token or authentication method specific to that endpoint.\n\nThe access-control-allow-headers shows both Authorization and X-API-Key are allowed, so let me test X-API-Key with the POST endpoint since that header might be the correct approach for write operations.\n\nLooking at the successful GET request more carefully, I used `-H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-agent-2026-secure-key\"` without quotes around the header value, but the Authorization header format should typically be `Bearer <token>`. The fact that this worked suggests the server might be checking for the token in a case-insensitive way or accepting it in an unexpected format.\n\nSince the Bearer authorization worked for the GET request, let me try applying the same header format to the POST request to see if it resolves the 401 error.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-f21ac55a-8c4f-443a-b380-34e2ed79e6db","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -v -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"threadId\":\"arbitration\",\"content\":\"test\"}' 2>&1 | grep -E \"(HTTP|Unauthorized|access-control)\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":34348,"output":541,"cacheRead":256,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":35145,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1778955473358,"responseId":"chatcmpl-f2a56682-bb35-4bb1-9703-f2bd3f2953d0"}}
