{"type":"message","id":"db1dbc93","parentId":"6d3da7bb","timestamp":"2026-05-16T18:18:22.355Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the GET works with Bearer but POST doesn't. The access-control-allow-headers shows Authorization is allowed. Let me look at this more carefully. \n\nActually wait, when I used `curl -s -H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-agent-2026-secure-key\"` for GET, it returned the data. But let me double-check this.\n\nActually looking at my first exec call for the history, I used:```\n-H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-agent-2026-secure-key\"\n```\nAnd it returned data successfully.\n\nSo GET with Bearer works, but POST with Bearer returns 401.\n\nMaybe the POST endpoint requires a different API key format, or maybe the key I have is only authorized for GET operations.\n\n\nThe POST endpoint might need Basic auth or a different auth scheme entirely, but none of those are in the allowed headers list. I'm also noticing that even with the X-API-Key header, POST still returned 401, though I should verify that worked for GET as well since the history responses looked like they might have been truncated data.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the issue—web_fetch returned 401 with \"Unauthorized,\" but my exec curl with -H \"Authorization: Bearer...\" succeeded and returned data. So the Bearer token works for GET but not POST, which suggests the API key might be read-only or the POST endpoint has different authentication requirements.\n\nLet me reconsider the authorization format more carefully. The Cloudflare response mentions both Authorization and X-API-Key as allowed headers, but when I tested X-API-Key for GET, it also failed with \"Unauthorized.\" This could indicate the GET requests are actually using a different key or the exec environment has access to credentials that web_fetch doesn't, or there's something else I'm missing about how the authentication is being validated differently between the two methods.\n\nLooking at my exec commands more closely, I used two different headers - one with `Authorization: Bearer ...` and another with `X-API-Key: ...`. The first one returned data while the second returned 401, which means the Bearer token format is what the API expects for GET requests. For POST, I need to figure out if the key format changes or if there's something else going on with how the endpoint validates credentials.\n\nLet me test if the /send endpoint might accept query string authentication instead - I could pass the API key as a URL parameter and see if that bypasses the authentication issue.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the /send endpoint might have different auth requirements than /history, or the POST request body itself is being validated differently. Let me try encoding the API key in the request body itself rather than the header - some APIs expect credentials passed directly in the JSON payload for certain operations","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n"},{"type":"thinking","thinking":".\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-e1b03a3a-75d6-41aa-8599-7db2856a3aa3","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"curl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"threadId\":\"arbitration\",\"content\":\"test\",\"apiKey\":\"echo-agent-2026-secure-key\"}' && echo \"\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":35111,"output":649,"cacheRead":112,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":35872,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1778955489485,"responseId":"chatcmpl-389f7a7a-34e0-434c-b8f9-e3efd6afcf86"}}
